{
  "passage": "The Sun is the main source of energy for the Earth, and solar energy can be turned directly into electricity using solar panels. A solar panel is made of many solar cells, usually made of silicon. When sunlight falls on a solar cell, it produces a small electric current. A single cell gives only about half a volt, so many cells are joined together in a panel. India receives plenty of sunshine, with about 300 sunny days a year in most parts of the country. A rooftop solar system of 1 kilowatt needs about 10 square metres of roof space and can produce about 4 units of electricity on a sunny day, which is enough to run a few lights, fans and a television in a small home. Solar energy does not produce smoke or harmful gases, and the sunlight itself costs nothing. However, solar panels produce no electricity at night and less on cloudy days, so the extra electricity produced during the day is stored in batteries or sent to the main electricity grid. The cost of solar panels has fallen sharply over the last fifteen years. The village of Dharnai in Bihar became one of the first in India to run entirely on a solar micro-grid in 2014. The Bhadla Solar Park in Rajasthan, spread over about 56 square kilometres, is one of the largest solar parks in the world.",
